introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do you structure data so it stays accurate as systems grow?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on relational data design. You will study how databases use tables, keys, and constraints to maintain <strong>integrity</strong> and reduce <strong>redundancy</strong>."
  journey: "You will explain the strengths and limits of relational databases, describe conceptual/logical/physical schema levels, explain how <strong>1NF</strong>, <strong>2NF</strong>, and <strong>3NF</strong> differ, and outline the role of SQL language types: <strong>DDL</strong> and <strong>DML</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Relational structure"
    icon: "fa-database"
    content: |
      <p>Relational systems store data in linked tables.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Primary keys</strong> identify records uniquely.</li>
          <li><strong>Foreign keys</strong> connect related tables.</li>
          <li><strong>Referential integrity</strong> prevents invalid relationships.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Schema levels"
    icon: "fa-layer-group"
    content: |
      <p>Schemas describe database architecture at different levels:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Conceptual:</strong> entities and relationships.</li>
          <li><strong>Logical:</strong> fields, keys, and constraints.</li>
          <li><strong>Physical:</strong> DBMS implementation and access strategy.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Normalization"
    icon: "fa-filter-circle-dollar"
    content: |
      <p>Normalization improves consistency and reduces anomalies:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>1NF:</strong> atomic values, no repeating groups.</li>
          <li><strong>2NF:</strong> no partial dependency on a composite key.</li>
          <li><strong>3NF:</strong> no transitive dependency between non-key fields.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "SQL language types"
    icon: "fa-code"
    content: |
      <p>SQL is grouped by purpose:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>DDL:</strong> define/modify structure (<code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>DML:</strong> query and update rows (<code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>).</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Relational Database"
    definition: "A database that stores linked data in tables using keys and constraints."
  - term: "Primary Key"
    definition: "A field or set of fields that uniquely identifies each record in a table."
  - term: "Foreign Key"
    definition: "A field that references a primary key in another table."
  - term: "Schema"
    definition: "The defined structure of a database and its relationships."
  - term: "Conceptual Schema"
    definition: "A high-level representation of entities and relationships."
  - term: "Logical Schema"
    definition: "Detailed table design including attributes, keys, and constraints."
  - term: "Physical Schema"
    definition: "How the database is implemented in a specific DBMS."
  - term: "Normalization"
    definition: "Organizing tables to reduce redundancy and improve integrity."
  - term: "1NF / 2NF / 3NF"
    definition: "Successive normal forms based on atomicity and dependency rules."
  - term: "DDL / DML"
    definition: "SQL categories for structure management and data manipulation."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Normalization is just sorting data"
    correction: "Normalization is a dependency-based redesign process, not row sorting."
  - mistake: "Cardinality and modality are the same"
    correction: "Cardinality describes maximum relationship count; modality describes minimum participation."
  - mistake: "Relational tables map directly to OOP classes"
    correction: "Relational models and object models overlap, but they are not equivalent structures."

syllabusPoints:
  - code: "A3.1.1"
    topic: "Relational database features, benefits, and limitations"
  - code: "A3.2.1"
    topic: "Database schema levels"
  - code: "A3.2.5"
    topic: "Differences between 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF"
  - code: "A3.3.1"
    topic: "SQL language types (DDL and DML)"

applications:
  - "School student information and timetable systems"
  - "Online ordering and stock tracking"
  - "Healthcare appointment databases"
  - "Banking account and transaction records"
  - "Library and borrowing systems"

thinkDeeper:
  question: "Can perfect structure ever capture messy real-world information?"
  context: "Database design improves consistency, but every schema is a model with assumptions and trade-offs."
